The literal `11` is a magic number whose meaning is not expressed in the code. It is implicitly `THEME_COLOR_LIMIT - 1` (the theme constant is `12`, the style generator produces `section--1` for the root and `section-0` through `section-10` for non-root nodes, giving 11 valid non-root values). If `THEME_COLOR_LIMIT` is ever raised in the theme files, this modulus will silently become wrong and the original visual bug will reappear for the newly-added colors.

Define a named constant directly above the method (or at the top of the file) that makes the intent explicit, e.g.:
```ts
// Number of distinct CSS section classes available for non-root nodes
// (styles.ts generates section-0 … section-(THEME_COLOR_LIMIT-2), i.e. 11 classes when THEME_COLOR_LIMIT === 12)
const SECTION_COLOR_COUNT = 11;
```
Then use `index % SECTION_COLOR_COUNT`. A comment linking it to `THEME_COLOR_LIMIT` makes the dependency auditable without requiring a cross-package import.

Using `as MindmapLayoutNode` on the result of `.find()` silently suppresses TypeScript's `T | undefined` warning. If a node is not found (e.g. because the label string doesn't match), `child1` will be `undefined` at runtime and the subsequent `expect(child1.section)` will throw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`, producing a confusing failure message rather than a clear test assertion failure.

Prefer an explicit existence check before the assertions:
```ts
const child1 = result.nodes.find((n) => n.label === '1');
expect(child1).toBeDefined();
// Now TypeScript knows it is defined, or use optional chaining:
expect(child1?.section).toBe(0);
```
This applies to all six `.find()` calls in the new test block.
